@page "/setup"
@using DYS.Molargo.Shared.Features.Setup.Components
@using DYS.Molargo.Services.Features.Setup
@using DYS.Molargo.Shared.Features.Setup.ViewModels
@inherits MvvmComponentBase<SetupViewModel>

@*
    What a practice does first, after signing up.

    A checklist rather than a wizard. A wizard owns the order and will not let anybody
    past step two, which is wrong here: a practice that wants to book a patient in before
    it has decided its opening hours should be able to, and the steps are genuinely
    independent of each other.

    Every step links out to the screen that already owns it instead of repeating its form.
    Two forms over one set of fields is two sets of rules that drift, and the first symptom
    is a value this screen accepts and the real screen refuses.

    Nothing is stored. Each step reads its state from the data every time the page opens,
    so a box cannot stay ticked after the thing it described was switched off.
*@

<PageTitle>Setup — Molargo</PageTitle>

@if (!ViewModel.IsPermitted)
{
    <div class="border-b-2 border-divider px-6 py-4">
        <h4 class="m-0">Setup</h4>
    </div>

    <div class="px-6 py-5">
        <p class="m-0 max-w-[60ch] text-sm text-ink/55">
            Setting the practice up is the owner's job, and every step on this list leads
            into Admin. Ask whoever set up your account.
        </p>
    </div>
}
else
{
    @if (ViewModel.HasError)
    {
        <NoticeComponent Kind="NoticeKind.Failure"
                         Headline="@ViewModel.ErrorHeadline"
                         Message="@ViewModel.ErrorMessage" />
    }

    @* ---- heading ---- *@
    <div class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline justify-between gap-x-4 gap-y-1 border-b-2 border-divider px-6 py-4">
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-3 gap-y-1">
            <h4 class="m-0">Setting up @ViewModel.PracticeName</h4>

            @if (ViewModel.SiteName is { Length: > 0 } site)
            {
                <span class="text-[13px] text-ink/55">@site</span>
            }
        </div>

        <div class="text-[13px] text-ink/55">
            @ViewModel.Tally@(ViewModel.TrialLine is { Length: > 0 } trial ? $" · {trial}" : "")
        </div>
    </div>

    @* ---- where we are ---- *@
    <div class="border-b-2 border-divider px-6 py-4">
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline justify-between gap-x-4 gap-y-1">
            <div class="font-heading text-lg font-extrabold">@ViewModel.Headline</div>

            <div class="text-xs text-ink/55">@($"{ViewModel.Percent}% complete")</div>
        </div>

        @*
            A bar, not a ring or a set of dots. The width is the one number it carries and
            it is readable at a glance from across a reception desk, which is where this
            screen gets used.

            Inline width because the value is a percentage of a run-time count; Tailwind
            emits only the literal classes it finds in source, so w-[@@percent%] would
            never reach the stylesheet.
        *@
        <div class="mt-2 h-1.5 w-full bg-neutral-200" role="presentation">
            <div class="h-full bg-accent" style="@($"width:{ViewModel.Percent}%")"></div>
        </div>

        <p class="mt-3 mb-0 max-w-[70ch] text-sm text-ink/70">
            Work through these in any order — nothing here blocks anything else. The
            optional ones are genuinely optional: a solo practice that never sends a text
            is a finished practice, not an unfinished one.
        </p>
    </div>

    @* ---- the steps ---- *@
    <StepGroupComponent Title="The practice"
                        Caption="Where you are and when you are open."
                        Steps="ViewModel.Practice" />

    <StepGroupComponent Title="People, and reaching them"
                        Caption="Who can sign in, and how patients hear from you."
                        Steps="ViewModel.People" />

    <StepGroupComponent Title="Going live"
                        Caption="The first real records. Nothing was invented for you."
                        Steps="ViewModel.Live" />

    @* ---- what arrived already ---- *@
    <div class="border-b-2 border-divider px-6 py-5">
        <h5 class="mt-0 mb-2">Already done for you</h5>

        <p class="max-w-[70ch] text-sm text-ink/70">
            @*
                Worth saying out loud. Somebody who has just been handed a checklist
                assumes everything else is empty too, and goes looking for a "set up the
                fee catalogue" step that does not exist because the catalogue is already
                there.
            *@
            This practice started with the medical history questionnaire, the consent
            forms, the message templates, the stock categories, the fee catalogue, the
            appointment types and the dental formulary already filled in. All of it is
            editable, and none of it is on this list — change it when it is wrong rather
            than before you start.
        </p>

        <p class="mt-3 mb-0 max-w-[70ch] text-sm text-ink/55">
            What did <em>not</em> arrive: patients, appointments and invoices. Inventing
            those would put fictional people in a real clinic's list, where the only way
            to find out which are fake is to open each one.
        </p>
    </div>

    @if (ViewModel.IsComplete)
    {
        <div class="border-b-2 border-divider px-6 py-5">
            <h5 class="mt-0 mb-2">That is the essential set</h5>

            <p class="max-w-[70ch] text-sm text-ink/70">
                The Setup link drops out of the top bar once the required steps are done.
                @* Said plainly, because a page somebody cannot find again is a page they
                   assume was deleted. *@
                This page stays at <span class="font-mono text-[13px]">/setup</span> if you
                want to come back to the optional ones.
            </p>
        </div>
    }

    <div class="px-6 pb-6 font-mono text-[11px] text-neutral-700">
        WEB · SETUP
    </div>
}
